// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["iii-a"] = {"qs": [["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "Before executing a delivery instruction slip (DIS) submitted in paper form, a DP should:", ["Verify the client's signature and that the slip was issued to that client", "Execute it immediately without any verification", "Accept it only if signed by the stock broker", "Ask SEBI to approve the transfer before it is processed in the depository system"], 0, "DPs must verify signatures and DIS serial numbers to prevent misuse of slips, and must keep records of instructions received."], ["Proxy Advisors", "A proxy advisor's voting recommendation is:", ["A legal opinion approved by SEBI", "Advisory in nature, and the institutional investor decides how to vote", "Binding on the institutional investor that subscribes to the proxy advisory service", "Binding on the company's board of directors"], 1, "Recommendations help investors decide but are not binding. Institutional investors remain responsible for their own voting decisions and must disclose them."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "Which of the following is NOT one of the actions SEBI may take against an intermediary under these regulations?", ["Cancellation of the certificate of registration", "Imprisonment of the intermediary's directors by SEBI", "Suspension of the certificate of registration", "Issue of a warning or prohibition from taking new clients"], 1, "SEBI can suspend or cancel registration, prohibit taking new clients, issue warnings and other directions. Imprisonment can be ordered only by a court in criminal proceedings."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Which regulator supervises the government securities market and the call money market?", ["Reserve Bank of India", "SEBI", "Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority", "Ministry of Corporate Affairs"], 0, "The RBI regulates the money market, the government securities market and the forex market. SEBI regulates trading in listed securities on stock exchanges."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Which stage of money laundering involves moving funds through many complex transactions to hide their origin?", ["Structuring of tax returns", "Layering", "Placement", "Integration"], 1, "Placement introduces illicit cash into the system, layering disguises the trail through multiple transactions, and integration brings the funds back into the economy as apparently legitimate."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "A credit rating decision at a CRA is taken by:", ["The issuer's board of directors", "A rating committee, based on analysis by the rating team", "The stock exchange where the debt is listed", "The marketing team that obtained the mandate"], 1, "Ratings are decided by a rating committee to ensure objectivity, separating rating decisions from business development and commercial considerations. Business teams cannot influence rating outcomes."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "A credit rating agency must keep a rating under surveillance during the life of the instrument. This means it must:", ["Review the rating at least once a year and whenever significant developments occur", "Review the rating only when the issuer requests it and pays a separate additional review fee", "Withdraw the rating after the first interest payment", "Never change a rating once assigned"], 0, "CRAs must continuously monitor ratings, conduct at least annual reviews and act promptly on material events that may affect creditworthiness."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "Which of the following is a typical activity of a merchant banker?", ["Settling trades as a clearing corporation", "Issuing insurance policies to investors", "Managing public issues, including drafting the offer document and coordinating intermediaries", "Accepting public deposits like a bank"], 2, "Merchant bankers manage issues, advise on capital structure, conduct due diligence and coordinate other intermediaries. Accepting deposits and settlement are not their functions."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "A CRA also offers non-rating services such as consulting. Under the regulations, it must:", ["Rate only companies that also buy its consulting services", "Let consultants sit on rating committees", "Offer discounted rating fees to all those issuers that also take its consulting services", "Keep these activities separate from rating activities to avoid conflicts of interest"], 3, "CRAs must segregate non-rating activities and manage conflicts so that commercial relationships do not influence ratings. Such separation protects the credibility of ratings."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "An RTA must maintain proper books and records for its clients and keep them:", ["Accessible to any member of the public on request", "Only in the issuer's registered office and never at the RTA's own premises", "Destroyed once the issue is complete", "Available for inspection by SEBI and preserved for the specified period"], 3, "RTAs must maintain records such as applications, allotment, transfers and grievances, preserve them for prescribed periods and produce them during SEBI inspections."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "A KRA must have adequate systems to ensure that KYC records are:", ["Destroyed immediately after an account is opened", "Available freely to any member of the public", "Stored only in paper form in fireproof cabinets at the head office", "Protected against unauthorised access, alteration or loss"], 3, "KRA Regulations require robust systems for data security, integrity and confidentiality, along with disaster recovery arrangements. Paper-only storage would not meet these standards."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "A CRA publishes a press release explaining the key factors behind a rating. This is called a:", ["Rating mandate", "Rating agreement", "Rating symbol", "Rating rationale"], 3, "CRAs must publish rating rationales covering the key drivers, strengths, weaknesses and outlook, so investors understand the basis of the rating."], ["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "Depositories send investors a Consolidated Account Statement (CAS) mainly to:", ["Show only the balance in the investor's linked savings bank account along with interest credited", "Calculate the investor's income-tax payable", "Give a single view of the investor's securities holdings and transactions across accounts", "Replace the need for contract notes from brokers"], 2, "CAS consolidates holdings and transactions across demat accounts and mutual fund folios linked by PAN, helping investors detect unauthorised activity."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "When an RTA processes a request for issue of duplicate securities, it now issues a:", ["Letter of confirmation, which the investor uses to get the securities dematerialised", "Fresh offer document to the investor", "New physical share certificate in all cases", "Cash refund equal to the market value of the lost securities on the date of the request"], 0, "For service requests like duplicates or transmission, RTAs issue a letter of confirmation, and the investor must submit it for dematerialisation within the validity period."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "An authorised person (AP) of a stock broker is:", ["An agent appointed by the broker, with exchange approval, to deal with clients on its behalf", "An independent broker with direct exchange membership", "A SEBI official who audits stock brokers and their branch offices on behalf of the stock exchanges", "A client with power of attorney over other clients"], 0, "APs act as agents of the broker and are approved by the stock exchange. The broker remains responsible for their acts."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A listed company must maintain a structured digital database (SDD) to record:", ["The voting results of all general meetings", "The salary details of all employees", "The dividend paid to every shareholder in the last ten years", "The names and PAN of persons with whom UPSI is shared, with time stamps"], 3, "The SDD records the nature of UPSI and details of persons sharing and receiving it, with time stamps and audit trails, so that leaks can be traced."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Corporatisation and demutualisation of a stock exchange means:", ["The exchange is converted into a government department", "The exchange stops trading in equity and moves to derivatives", "Ownership and management are separated from the trading rights of members", "Only brokers may own shares in the exchange"], 2, "Demutualisation separates owners from traders so that brokers' trading interests do not control the exchange, improving governance and reducing conflicts of interest."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "A registered investment adviser must get an annual compliance audit conducted. The audit is meant to:", ["Certify the adviser's investment returns for marketing", "Approve the adviser's personal investments", "Check the adviser's compliance with the IA Regulations and SEBI circulars", "Calculate the adviser's income-tax liability"], 2, "The annual audit, conducted by a qualified auditor, verifies adherence to the regulations and circulars, and its findings must be acted upon."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Which of the following is a typical red flag of possible money laundering in a broking account?", ["A client choosing to receive contract notes electronically", "Large funds deposited and withdrawn soon after with little trading activity", "A client updating his correspondence address after relocating to another city for work", "A salaried client investing a small amount every month"], 1, "Funds routed through an account with little genuine trading suggest the account is being used to move money, which should be examined for an STR."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "Under the margin pledge mechanism, when a client provides shares as margin, the shares:", ["Are sold by the broker and the cash is kept as margin", "Remain in the client's demat account and are pledged in favour of the broker", "Are transferred to the broker's own demat account", "Are held in physical form by the broker"], 1, "Since 2020, securities given as collateral must be pledged and re-pledged rather than transferred to the broker, keeping them in the client's account."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "A research analyst uses an AI tool to generate parts of research reports for clients. Under SEBI's framework, the analyst is:", ["Prohibited from using any software in research", "Required to get SEBI's prior approval for every report that is prepared with such tools", "Fully responsible for the output and must disclose the use of such tools to clients", "Not responsible for errors made by the AI tool"], 2, "SEBI's amended framework makes analysts solely responsible for data security, confidentiality and accuracy of output generated using AI tools, with disclosure to clients."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "Which of the following is a function of a registrar to an issue?", ["Finalising the basis of allotment along with the lead manager and stock exchange", "Acting as the custodian of FPI investments", "Rating the debt instruments issued by the company and publishing the rating rationale", "Underwriting the unsubscribed portion of the issue"], 0, "The registrar processes applications, finalises the basis of allotment with the lead manager and exchange, and handles allotment and refund instructions."], ["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "Which of the following entities is eligible to apply for registration as a depository participant?", ["A listed manufacturing company", "A proprietary concern with no financial business", "A scheduled commercial bank", "An individual retail investor"], 2, "Banks, financial institutions, custodians, clearing corporations, stock brokers and NBFCs meeting criteria can become DPs. Ordinary companies and individuals are not eligible."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "If an issuer defaults on interest payments, the debenture trustee should:", ["Wait until maturity before taking any action", "Reduce the interest rate on behalf of holders", "Resign immediately and appoint a new trustee", "Inform debenture holders, seek their consent and take steps to enforce the security"], 3, "On default, the trustee must notify holders, convene meetings, follow their decisions and take action, such as enforcing security or entering inter-creditor arrangements."], ["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "A Basic Services Demat Account (BSDA) is designed for:", ["Companies holding treasury shares", "Small investors with low holdings, offering reduced or nil annual maintenance charges", "Foreign portfolio investors only, who need a low-cost account for their custodial holdings", "Clearing members holding pool securities"], 1, "BSDA aims to reduce the cost of holding securities for small investors, with lower or zero annual maintenance charges up to specified holding values."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "An NBFC that is also registered as a stock broker would be supervised by:", ["RBI alone, since it is primarily a finance company", "The Ministry of Corporate Affairs, since it supervises all companies registered in India", "SEBI alone, since securities activities override other licences", "RBI for its NBFC activities and SEBI for its stock broking activities"], 3, "India follows activity-based regulation, so an entity with multiple activities answers to each relevant regulator for that activity. Each regulator inspects the activity under its law."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "Under the RA Regulations, a research analyst must maintain records of research reports and the basis of recommendations so that:", ["The analyst can claim tax deductions", "The analyst can avoid giving disclosures in reports", "The rationale for each recommendation can be verified during inspection", "Clients can resell the reports to other investors for a fee at a later date"], 2, "Analysts must keep records of research, the data used and the rationale, and make them available to SEBI or the supervisory body during inspections."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "For a mutual fund, SEBI rules restrict the appointment of a custodian that is:", ["Registered with SEBI for over five years", "An associate in which the sponsor or its associates hold a controlling stake", "Based in a different city from the asset management company's registered head office", "A scheduled commercial bank"], 1, "To protect independence, a custodian in which the sponsor or its associates hold 50% or more of voting rights generally cannot be the fund's custodian."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "A beneficial owner wants to pledge his demat shares as security for a loan. Under the Depositories Act, he:", ["Needs prior approval from SEBI for each pledge", "Cannot pledge demat shares at all", "Must first convert the shares into physical form", "Can create the pledge through the depository, following prescribed procedures"], 3, "Section 12 allows beneficial owners to pledge or hypothecate securities held in a depository, with the pledge recorded by the depository."], ["Proxy Advisors", "A proxy advisor provides consulting services to a company and also issues voting recommendations on that company's resolutions. This raises a concern of:", ["Failure to file an offer document with SEBI before advising", "Insider trading by the company's shareholders", "Conflict of interest, which must be disclosed and managed", "Market manipulation under PFUTP"], 2, "Providing consulting to a company while advising investors on its resolutions can bias recommendations, so such conflicts must be disclosed and managed."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "Issuers of listed debt securities must create a Recovery Expense Fund. This fund is used to:", ["Pay the debenture trustee's annual fees", "Meet expenses incurred by the debenture trustee for enforcement and recovery on default", "Buy back the debentures at a premium from holders who want to exit well before maturity", "Pay interest to debenture holders every quarter"], 1, "The Recovery Expense Fund, kept with the stock exchange, finances legal and enforcement action by the trustee if the issuer defaults."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "Before providing investment advice, a registered investment adviser must:", ["Enter into an investment advisory agreement with the client", "Collect fees for five years in advance", "Obtain the client's demat account password", "Get the client's employer to approve the advice"], 0, "The regulations require an advisory agreement setting out terms, fees and the scope of services before advice is given. The agreement also covers fees and termination terms."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "Who among the following is generally exempt from registration as an investment adviser?", ["An individual charging fees for stock-specific advice via an app", "A firm giving portfolio advice to the public for a fixed fee", "A person giving advice exclusively on insurance products regulated by IRDAI", "A company providing comprehensive financial planning services to families for a fee"], 2, "Persons advising only on products regulated by another regulator, like insurance agents on IRDAI products, are exempt. Paid securities advice generally needs registration."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "A firm with an effective compliance culture is most likely to show which feature?", ["Compliance is discussed only when an inspection is announced", "Business targets override compliance checks during busy periods", "Breaches are fixed quietly without any record being kept", "Staff report concerns freely and senior management acts on them"], 3, "A healthy compliance culture is driven from the top, encourages escalation of issues without fear and treats compliance as part of everyday business."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Under the PMLA, a reporting entity applies a risk-based approach. This means it should:", ["Apply due diligence checks only to clients who invest more than ₹1 crore in a financial year", "Apply due diligence measures in proportion to the money laundering risk each client poses", "Treat all clients as high risk and reject most applications", "Leave risk assessment to FIU-IND"], 1, "Clients are categorised as low, medium or high risk, and the depth of due diligence and monitoring is matched to that risk."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "Investor service requests such as change of address for physical holders are submitted to the RTA using:", ["Standardised forms prescribed by SEBI for investor service requests", "A request to the depository participant only", "An application to the Registrar of Companies", "A plain letter to the stock exchange"], 0, "SEBI has standardised ISR forms for physical holders to update PAN, KYC, nomination and other details with RTAs, simplifying the process."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "Following the post-issue period, the lead merchant banker is responsible for:", ["Acting as the issuer's statutory auditor", "Running the company's day-to-day operations", "Monitoring allotment, refunds, listing and other post-issue activities until completion", "Nothing, as its role ends on the issue closing date"], 2, "Post-issue, the lead manager coordinates with the registrar, bankers and exchanges to ensure timely allotment, refunds, unblocking and listing. Its responsibility ends only after these steps are complete."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "Which of the following persons generally needs registration as a research analyst?", ["A person who publishes research reports with buy or sell recommendations for consideration", "A company publishing its own audited results", "A journalist writing a general market news report", "An investor discussing his own trades with family"], 0, "Registration is needed by persons preparing research reports or making recommendations on securities as a business. General news and personal discussions are outside the scope."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "Compliance risk for an intermediary is best described as the risk of:", ["Legal or regulatory sanctions, financial loss or reputational damage from failing to follow laws, rules and codes", "A breakdown of computer systems at the stock exchange", "Losing money because market prices move against the firm's own positions", "A counterparty failing to pay what it owes on settlement day"], 0, "Compliance risk arises from not complying with laws, regulations, codes of conduct and internal standards. Market, credit and operational risk are separate categories."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "A person facing SEBI proceedings applies to settle them by paying a settlement amount without admitting or denying guilt. This is possible under:", ["The SCRR, 1957", "The Depositories Act, 1996", "The settlement mechanism under Section 15JB of the SEBI Act", "The compounding provisions of the Income-tax Act, 1961 for tax offences"], 2, "Section 15JB allows settlement of administrative and civil proceedings on payment of settlement amount and other terms, subject to SEBI's settlement regulations."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "A company issues equity shares to a select group of investors on a private basis that is not a rights or public issue. Under ICDR this is a:", ["Bonus issue", "Rights issue", "Offer for sale", "Preferential issue"], 3, "A preferential issue is an issue to selected persons on a private placement basis, governed by Chapter V of ICDR including pricing and lock-in rules."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "An intermediary keeps a regulatory calendar listing every report and filing due date. This mainly helps to:", ["Increase the firm's brokerage income", "Reduce the minimum net worth the firm must maintain", "Replace the need for a compliance officer", "Avoid missed or delayed regulatory submissions"], 3, "A compliance calendar tracks due dates for reports, returns and renewals so that filings are made on time, a common area of lapses and penalties."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "An employee of a mutual fund buys a stock just before the fund places a large order in it, using his knowledge of the order. Under PFUTP this is:", ["Legitimate personal investment", "Front running", "Insider trading of UPSI of a listed company", "Index rebalancing"], 1, "Trading ahead of a large non-public order with knowledge of it is front running, which is covered under PFUTP regardless of whether the person is an intermediary."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "An issuer stops giving information to its credit rating agency. The CRA should:", ["Upgrade the rating to encourage cooperation", "Delete the rating from its records without disclosure", "Keep the rating unchanged indefinitely until the issuer resumes sharing information with it", "Flag the issuer as 'not cooperating' and review the rating based on available information"], 3, "Where an issuer is non-cooperative, the CRA must disclose this, flag the rating as 'issuer not cooperating' and rate based on available information."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "Under the PML Rules, besides the Principal Officer, a reporting entity must appoint a:", ["Chief Investment Officer to approve all large transactions of high-value clients", "Statutory auditor to file STRs", "Designated Director to ensure overall compliance with PMLA obligations", "Public relations officer for FIU-IND"], 2, "The Designated Director, typically the managing director or a whole-time director, is responsible for overall compliance with PMLA obligations. Both officers' details must be shared with FIU-IND."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "The trading window for designated persons of a listed company is ordinarily closed from the end of every quarter until:", ["Seven days before the board meeting that approves the results", "The first day of the next quarter", "The annual general meeting of the company", "48 hours after the declaration of financial results"], 3, "Trading restriction for designated persons generally runs from the end of the quarter until 48 hours after the financial results are made public."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "A custodian provides custodial services for gold held by a gold ETF. This is:", ["Prohibited, as custodians can hold only shares", "Allowed only if the gold is held abroad", "Permitted, as custodial services can extend to goods such as gold held by mutual funds", "Allowed only for foreign investors"], 2, "Custodians may provide safekeeping for goods such as gold or silver held by mutual fund schemes, in addition to securities."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "An FPI's holding in a listed Indian company crosses the individual investment limit. The FPI must:", ["Seek approval from the company's board to keep it", "Convert the shares into debentures", "Divest the excess within the prescribed time or have the entire holding treated as FDI", "Retain the holding without any further action"], 2, "If an FPI breaches the limit, it may either sell down the excess within the specified period or have its total investment reclassified as foreign direct investment."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Which body may, under the SCRA, supersede the governing board of a recognised stock exchange in public interest?", ["The company with the largest market capitalisation on the exchange", "The Registrar of Companies", "The stock exchange's largest broker", "SEBI, exercising powers delegated by the Central Government"], 3, "Section 11 of the SCRA allows supersession of a stock exchange's governing board in the interest of the trade or the public. This power is exercised by SEBI."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Which of the following is most likely to be classified as a Category I FPI?", ["A family office", "An unregulated corporate body incorporated in a tax haven", "An individual resident in a foreign country", "A pension fund regulated in a FATF member country"], 3, "Category I covers government and related investors, pension funds, and appropriately regulated entities from FATF member jurisdictions. Individuals, family offices and corporates fall in Category II."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "Under the Custodian Regulations, a custodian must have adequate internal controls. A key reason is to:", ["Allow the custodian to lend client securities without consent", "Prevent loss, misuse or unauthorised use of client assets", "Enable the custodian to trade on clients' behalf", "Replace the need for audits"], 1, "Internal controls, audits and checks protect client assets from errors, fraud and misuse, which is the custodian's core responsibility. Internal and external audits test these controls regularly."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Under the SCRA, 1956, which of the following is included in the definition of 'securities'?", ["A residential property title deed", "A life insurance policy", "Units of a mutual fund scheme", "A savings bank deposit"], 2, "Section 2(h) of the SCRA includes shares, bonds, derivatives, government securities, units of mutual funds and collective investment schemes, and security receipts. Bank deposits and insurance policies are not securities."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "Under the merchant banker framework, a lead manager must ensure that the advertisements for a public issue:", ["Are released before SEBI's observations to build demand", "Highlight only positive aspects without risk factors", "Promise a guaranteed listing gain to attract retail investors", "Are consistent with the offer document and not misleading"], 3, "Issue advertisements must be truthful, fair and consistent with the offer document, and must not contain misleading statements or promises of returns."], ["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "A client wants to prevent any debit from his demat account while he is abroad. He can request his DP to:", ["Transfer all shares to the DP's account", "Convert all holdings into physical form", "Close the account permanently", "Freeze the account for debits"], 3, "Clients can freeze their demat accounts for debits, credits or both, which protects against unauthorised transfers during the period. The client can revoke the freeze when he returns."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "In a book-built IPO, anchor investors are:", ["Retail investors applying for less than ₹2 lakh", "Qualified institutional buyers who are allocated shares a day before the issue opens", "Employees applying under the employee reservation portion at a discount to the issue price", "Promoters contributing to the minimum promoter contribution"], 1, "Anchor investors are QIBs who bid one working day before the issue opens, receive allocation from the QIB portion and are subject to lock-in."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "Which document typically sets out an intermediary's compliance responsibilities, procedures and escalation paths?", ["The annual income-tax return", "A board-approved compliance policy or manual", "The memorandum and articles of association of the firm", "The client registration form"], 1, "A compliance policy or manual approved by the board lays out roles, procedures, monitoring and reporting, guiding staff on how to meet regulatory requirements."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "A KRA validates a client's KYC record by checking it against official sources such as the Income Tax Department database. The purpose of this validation is to:", ["Approve the client's trading limit", "Calculate the client's tax liability", "Determine the client's eligibility for IPO allotment", "Confirm key attributes like PAN, name and address so the record can be relied upon"], 3, "KRAs independently validate records against official databases so that intermediaries can rely on them, which also supports portability of KYC."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "Under SEBI norms, an issuer's failure to pay principal or interest even by one day beyond the due date is generally recognised by CRAs as:", ["A reason to upgrade the rating", "A default", "A minor delay with no rating impact", "A technical error that can be ignored"], 1, "CRAs follow a uniform default recognition policy in which even a one-day delay in servicing debt is treated as a default unless specific conditions apply."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "Which of these would create a conflict of interest that bars an entity from acting as debenture trustee for an issue?", ["Being a scheduled commercial bank", "Being an associate of the issuer company", "Having long experience in debenture trusteeship", "Having its registered office in Mumbai"], 1, "An entity cannot be a debenture trustee for an issuer it is associated with, since it must act independently for the debenture holders."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "Which right does a beneficial owner have for securities held through a depository?", ["Only voting rights, while the dividends are paid to the depository participant", "All rights and benefits, such as voting and dividends, and all liabilities", "No rights until the shares are rematerialised", "Only the right to sell, with dividends going to the depository"], 1, "Section 10(3) gives the beneficial owner all rights, benefits and liabilities attached to the securities held by a depository. The depository itself holds no such rights."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "A compliance officer finds a serious breach of client fund rules and senior management asks him to keep it quiet. What should he do?", ["Wait for the next inspection to see if it is noticed", "Escalate it to the board and report to the regulator as required", "Resolve it informally with the affected clients and close the matter without recording it", "Keep it confidential to protect the firm's reputation"], 1, "Compliance officers must report non-compliance to the board and to the regulator where rules require it. Hiding a breach exposes the firm and the officer to action."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "A 'maker-checker' control in an intermediary's back office means:", ["The same person enters and approves a transaction to save time", "Transactions are checked only after the year-end audit", "Clients check their own transactions and confirm them online", "One person enters a transaction and a different person verifies and approves it"], 3, "Maker-checker separates entry and approval between two people, reducing errors and fraud. It is a basic preventive control. It is widely used for payments and client fund transfers."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "An investor completes KYC with a stock broker and later opens an account with a mutual fund distributor. Because of the KRA system, the investor:", ["Must visit SEBI's office to transfer the KYC", "Need not repeat the full KYC process, as the second intermediary can fetch the KYC record from the KRA", "Must complete full KYC again with every new intermediary", "Can invest only after the broker gives written consent"], 1, "KRAs hold KYC records centrally so that an investor's KYC done once can be relied upon by other SEBI-registered intermediaries, avoiding duplication."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "Under the SEBI Act, which of these is an investigative power SEBI can use?", ["Freezing every bank account in the country during an inquiry", "Calling for information and records from intermediaries and persons associated with the securities market", "Ordering the arrest of any market participant without a court warrant", "Changing the tax rate on securities transactions"], 1, "SEBI can call for information, inspect books, summon persons and examine records. Search and seizure need approval of a designated court, and SEBI has no power to arrest."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "When a household deposits money in a bank and the bank lends it to a business, the process is called:", ["Securitisation", "Financial intermediation", "Disintermediation", "Dematerialisation"], 1, "The bank stands between savers and borrowers, pooling deposits and lending them on, which is financial intermediation. Disintermediation is the opposite, where borrowers raise funds directly from investors."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "If an intermediary finds that a client's KYC details have changed, such as a new address, it should:", ["Collect supporting documents and update the record in the KRA system", "Report the change only to the stock exchange", "Ignore the change until the annual review", "Close the existing account and open a fresh account with new KYC documents"], 0, "Intermediaries must update changes in KYC details with the KRA so that the central record stays accurate for all intermediaries relying on it."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A person who was a statutory auditor of a company four months ago, and had access to its UPSI, trades in its shares. Under the regulations he is:", ["A connected person, since the association was within six months before the act", "Not an insider, because his engagement has ended", "Exempt, as auditors are outside the scope of these rules", "An insider only if he currently holds over 10% of the shares"], 0, "A connected person includes anyone associated with the company in a way giving access to UPSI during the six months prior to the act concerned."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "Which of the following is the best example of a capital market instrument?", ["A ten-year corporate bond", "A three-month commercial paper", "An overnight call money loan", "A 91-day treasury bill"], 0, "Capital market instruments have maturities above one year and fund long-term needs. Treasury bills, commercial paper and call money are short-term money market instruments."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "A clearing corporation in the securities market mainly:", ["Sets interest rates for margin funding", "Issues contract notes to retail clients", "Clears and settles trades and acts as the central counterparty", "Lists securities and approves offer documents"], 2, "A recognised clearing corporation clears and settles exchange trades, guarantees settlement as central counterparty and manages the settlement guarantee fund."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "After SEBI cancels an intermediary's certificate of registration, the intermediary must generally:", ["Change its name and resume business without any new approval", "Stop the registered activity and protect the interests of existing clients as directed", "Continue taking new clients until the appeal is decided", "Transfer client funds to its own accounts for safekeeping"], 1, "On cancellation, the intermediary must cease the activity and allow clients to move, for example transferring records and assets, in line with SEBI's directions."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "The preamble of the SEBI Act, 1992 states SEBI's objectives as:", ["Collecting taxes on capital gains made in the securities market", "Fixing the prices at which shares are issued to the public", "Protecting investors' interests in securities and developing and regulating the securities market", "Regulating banks and controlling inflation in the economy"], 2, "SEBI was set up to protect investors, develop the securities market and regulate it. Monetary policy and banks are RBI's domain, and SEBI does not fix issue prices or collect taxes."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "Before a secured debt issue opens, the debenture trustee must:", ["Underwrite the issue to ensure full subscription", "Fix the coupon rate of the debentures", "Allot the debentures to investors and dispatch the allotment advice to each successful applicant", "Carry out due diligence on the assets proposed to be charged and the adequacy of security"], 3, "Debenture trustees must exercise independent due diligence to confirm that assets are free and adequate to create the stated security before the issue."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "A company raises fresh capital by issuing shares to investors for the first time. In which segment does this transaction take place?", ["Primary market", "Money market", "Secondary market", "Over-the-counter derivatives market"], 0, "Fresh issue of securities by an issuer, where money flows to the company, happens in the primary market. In the secondary market, existing securities change hands between investors and the issuer receives nothing."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "A research analyst is asked to take part in a roadshow to market an IPO for which his firm is the lead manager. Under the regulations he should:", ["Participate and present a buy recommendation", "Participate only if paid separately by the issuer", "Participate after informing the stock exchange and the issuer", "Not participate in the marketing or roadshow of the issue"], 3, "Analysts must not take part in business solicitation or marketing activities such as roadshows for issues handled by their firm, as this compromises independence."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "The administration and supervisory body for investment advisers, recognised by SEBI, is responsible for:", ["Fixing the fees that advisers can charge", "Registering new mutual fund schemes before they are launched to investors", "Paying compensation to all clients of advisers", "Supervising registered advisers, including inspection and monitoring"], 3, "SEBI recognised a stock exchange subsidiary as the administration and supervisory body for IAs, which handles supervision, inspection and related functions."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "A person who knowingly fails to pay a penalty imposed by an adjudicating officer and does not comply with SEBI's directions may face:", ["Recovery proceedings, including attachment of bank accounts and property, by a SEBI recovery officer", "Transfer of the matter to the Reserve Bank for recovery", "Only a warning letter, since penalties imposed by an adjudicating officer cannot be enforced against assets", "Automatic waiver of the penalty after one year"], 0, "Section 28A empowers SEBI's recovery officers to recover unpaid penalties and amounts through attachment and sale of assets and bank accounts, and arrest in some cases."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "A listed company raises funds by issuing shares only to qualified institutional buyers through a placement document. This route is called:", ["Employee stock option scheme", "Qualified Institutions Placement (QIP)", "Rights issue", "Initial Public Offer"], 1, "A QIP allows listed companies to raise capital quickly from QIBs under Chapter VI of ICDR without filing a draft offer document with SEBI for observations."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "Debenture holders want the trustee to convene a meeting to discuss a breach of covenant. The trustee must call a meeting when requested by holders representing at least:", ["Every single debenture holder", "One-tenth of the outstanding amount of debentures", "Half of the outstanding amount of debentures", "Three-fourths of the outstanding amount of debentures"], 1, "The regulations require the trustee to call a meeting of debenture holders on a requisition by holders of at least one-tenth in value of the outstanding debentures."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "A stock exchange in India can operate legally only if it:", ["Is registered as a non-banking finance company", "Is approved by the Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "Has at least ten listed companies", "Is recognised under the SCRA"], 3, "Recognition under Section 4 of the SCRA is mandatory for a stock exchange. The Central Government's power to grant recognition is exercised by SEBI."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "An investor holding physical shares of a listed company wants to transfer them to a buyer. Under current rules:", ["The company must issue new physical certificates to the buyer", "The RTA will register the physical transfer in the same way as before", "The buyer must apply to SEBI for permission", "Transfers of listed securities can be effected only in demat form"], 3, "Since April 2019, transfers of listed securities other than transmission or transposition are processed only in demat form, so physical holders must dematerialise first."], ["Proxy Advisors", "Which of the following best explains why SEBI regulates proxy advisors?", ["They act as stock exchanges for voting rights", "They hold investors' money in escrow", "Their recommendations can significantly influence voting outcomes and corporate governance", "They decide the listing of companies"], 2, "Because institutional investors often follow proxy advice, proxy advisors affect governance outcomes, which is why SEBI requires transparency and conflict management."], ["Proxy Advisors", "A company disagrees with a proxy advisor's recommendation and sends its comments. The proxy advisor should:", ["Report the company to SEBI for interference", "Withdraw its recommendation automatically", "Share the company's comments with its clients along with the report", "Delete the comments to avoid confusing clients"], 2, "Proxy advisors must have a process to receive company comments and communicate them to clients, so that investors see both views."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "Which pair correctly matches the market with the typical maturity of its instruments?", ["Money market - more than five years; capital market - up to one year", "Money market - exactly one year; capital market - exactly ten years", "Money market - up to one year; capital market - more than one year", "Money market and capital market - no difference in maturity"], 2, "The money market deals in short-term funds with maturity up to one year, while the capital market deals in long-term instruments such as equity and bonds."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "A broker executes a trade for a client without the client's instructions. This is:", ["Acceptable during volatile market conditions", "Permitted if the trade makes a profit and the client is informed later", "Allowed if the client had a debit balance", "Unauthorised trading, a violation of the broker's obligations"], 3, "Brokers must trade only on clients' instructions. Unauthorised trades breach the code of conduct and can lead to disciplinary action."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "Who carries ultimate responsibility for an intermediary's compliance with regulations?", ["The statutory auditor", "The compliance officer alone", "The stock exchange or depository where it is a member", "The board of directors and senior management"], 3, "The compliance officer advises, monitors and reports, but the board and senior management are ultimately accountable for ensuring the firm complies."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Which body under the Ministry of Finance is responsible for capital market policy matters and legislation such as the SEBI Act and the SCRA?", ["Department of Economic Affairs", "Department of Revenue", "Department of Expenditure", "Department of Financial Services"], 0, "The Department of Economic Affairs, through its capital markets division, handles policy and legislation relating to the securities market, while SEBI is the market regulator."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "A stock exchange refuses to list a company's securities. Under the SCRA, the company can appeal against this decision to:", ["The National Company Law Tribunal", "The Registrar of Companies", "The Securities Appellate Tribunal", "The Reserve Bank of India"], 2, "Section 22 of the SCRA lets a company appeal to SAT when a recognised stock exchange refuses, omits or fails to list its securities."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "An investment adviser wants to help a client invest in mutual funds recommended in its advice. Under the regulations, the adviser may provide implementation services:", ["Through regular plans to earn trail commission", "Only in schemes of the adviser's associate AMC", "Only through direct plans, without earning any commission", "Only if the client separately pays an extra commission to the adviser"], 2, "Investment advisers may offer implementation through direct plans of mutual funds, and cannot receive commission or consideration from product providers."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "For listed companies, provisions of the Companies Act, 2013 relating to issue and transfer of securities and non-payment of dividend are administered by:", ["SEBI", "The Reserve Bank of India", "The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India", "The Registrar of Companies alone"], 0, "Section 24 of the Companies Act, 2013 gives SEBI power to administer provisions on issue and transfer of securities and non-payment of dividend for listed companies and those intending to list."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "A research entity also has an investment banking division. To protect the independence of research, it must:", ["Maintain an effective separation (Chinese wall) between research and investment banking", "Publish research only on companies that are its banking clients", "Link each analyst's pay to the revenue earned from specific investment banking deals it covers", "Allow investment bankers to approve research reports before release"], 0, "The regulations require separation of research from investment banking and sales, and bar analysts' compensation from depending on specific banking transactions."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Under the FPI Regulations, 2019, an applicant applies for FPI registration through:", ["The Reserve Bank of India directly", "The Ministry of External Affairs", "Any SEBI-registered stock broker in India with a foreign client desk", "A Designated Depository Participant (DDP) approved by SEBI"], 3, "Registration of FPIs is granted by DDPs on behalf of SEBI. DDPs also carry out KYC and ongoing monitoring of FPIs."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "A registered investment adviser should disclose to clients:", ["Any conflict of interest, including any consideration received from associates", "Its competitors' fee structures", "The names of its other clients", "Only the positive outcomes of past advice"], 0, "Advisers must disclose material conflicts, such as associate relationships or interests in recommended products, so clients can evaluate the advice."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "A debenture trustee must disclose information such as defaults and breach of covenants to:", ["No one, as such information is confidential", "Only the Registrar of Companies", "Only the issuer's statutory auditor", "The stock exchanges and debenture holders, as required"], 3, "Trustees must report defaults, breaches and actions taken to the stock exchanges and keep debenture holders informed, as part of their transparency obligations."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Derivatives are treated as 'securities' under the SCRA. As a result, trading in derivatives is legal when it is:", ["Approved in writing by the company whose shares are the underlying of the derivative", "Settled only in cash through a bank", "Traded on a recognised stock exchange under its rules and bye-laws", "Agreed between any two persons, with no further conditions"], 2, "Section 18A makes derivative contracts legal and valid if traded on a recognised stock exchange and settled on its clearing house as per its rules and bye-laws."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "For a client that is a company, the PML Rules identify a beneficial owner as a natural person who, alone or together, holds a controlling ownership interest of more than:", ["51% of shares, capital or profits", "10% of shares, capital or profits", "75% of shares, capital or profits", "1% of shares, capital or profits"], 1, "Following the 2023 amendment to the PML Rules, the threshold for controlling ownership interest in a company is more than 10% of shares, capital or profits."], ["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "Under the D&P Regulations, a depository is required to:", ["Leave supervision of DPs entirely to SEBI", "Allow DPs to operate without any agreement", "Guarantee all trades executed on stock exchanges", "Conduct periodic inspection of its participants and act on deficiencies found"], 3, "Depositories act as first-level regulators of DPs, inspecting them, monitoring compliance and taking action under their bye-laws. SEBI also inspects depositories and DPs directly."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "An intermediary proposes a change in control. Under the Intermediaries Regulations, it must:", ["Surrender its existing registration and apply afresh as a new entity in every case", "Simply update its website, as no approval is needed", "Obtain SEBI's prior approval before the change in control takes effect", "Inform SEBI within one year after the change is completed"], 2, "A change in control of a registered intermediary requires SEBI's prior approval, so that the incoming controllers can be assessed for the fit and proper criteria."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "An appeal against a SEBI order must normally be filed with the Securities Appellate Tribunal within:", ["Any time, as there is no time limit", "Seven days from the date the order is received", "45 days from the date the order is received", "One year from the date of the order"], 2, "Section 15T allows 45 days for filing an appeal with SAT, which may admit a late appeal if there was sufficient cause for the delay."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "When SEBI proposes action against an intermediary, the intermediary receives a show cause notice. Its main purpose is to:", ["Ask the intermediary to pay the penalty immediately", "Inform the public and the stock exchanges that the intermediary has already been found guilty", "Give the intermediary an opportunity to respond before an order is passed", "Transfer the intermediary's clients to another firm"], 2, "A show cause notice follows principles of natural justice, letting the intermediary reply to the allegations before the designated authority's recommendation and final order."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "A mutual fund distributor describes himself as an 'independent financial adviser' on his visiting card. This is:", ["Permitted as long as he holds a valid ARN and has passed the relevant NISM distributor exam", "Permitted after informing his AMC", "Permitted if he advises fewer than 100 clients", "Not permitted, as the term 'adviser' is restricted to registered investment advisers"], 3, "SEBI prohibits distributors who are not registered as investment advisers from using terms like 'adviser' or 'IFA', to avoid misleading investors."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "Which of the following is a function of a financial system rather than of a single intermediary?", ["Issuing a contract note to one trading client", "Collecting the premium on one insurance policy", "Price discovery through the interaction of many buyers and sellers", "Maintaining a particular client's demat account"], 2, "Price discovery emerges from the collective interaction of market participants. The other options are specific services provided by individual intermediaries."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "A beneficial owner wishes to opt out of the depository system for some securities. The process is called:", ["Dematerialisation", "Consolidation", "Sub-division", "Rematerialisation"], 3, "Under Section 14, an investor can opt out of a depository, and the issuer then issues physical certificates. This reverse process is rematerialisation."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Which regulator issues directions on foreign exchange transactions under FEMA, 1999?", ["Competition Commission of India", "Reserve Bank of India", "SEBI", "Ministry of Corporate Affairs"], 1, "FEMA is administered mainly by the RBI, which frames regulations and issues directions on foreign exchange transactions, in consultation with the Central Government."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "A person aggrieved by an order of a SEBI adjudicating officer wants to challenge it. To which forum should he first appeal?", ["National Company Law Tribunal", "Supreme Court of India", "Securities Appellate Tribunal", "Central Government"], 2, "Orders of SEBI and its adjudicating officers are appealable to the Securities Appellate Tribunal. Only after a SAT order does an appeal lie to the Supreme Court."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "Which client typically must appoint a SEBI-registered custodian before investing in India?", ["A salaried employee investing via SIP", "A resident individual buying shares through a broker", "A foreign portfolio investor", "A retail investor applying in an IPO"], 2, "FPIs are required to appoint a custodian for safekeeping, settlement and regulatory reporting. Retail investors use DPs instead. DPs serve resident retail investors instead."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Which entity typically provides custody services to an FPI investing in India?", ["A SEBI-registered custodian", "The Securities Appellate Tribunal", "A KYC Registration Agency", "The issuer company itself"], 0, "FPIs must appoint a SEBI-registered custodian for safekeeping of securities and reporting of transactions to regulators. The custodian also reports the FPI's holdings to regulators."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "Under ICDR, equity shares held by persons other than promoters before an IPO are generally:", ["Locked in for ten years", "Locked in for six months from the date of allotment in the IPO", "Compulsorily sold in the offer for sale", "Free from any lock-in"], 1, "Pre-issue shareholding of non-promoters is generally locked in for six months from IPO allotment, subject to specified exemptions. Promoter shareholding is subject to separate, longer lock-in rules."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "Under the Intermediaries Regulations, every intermediary must appoint a compliance officer who is responsible for:", ["Fixing the brokerage rates charged to all clients", "Monitoring compliance with the Act, rules and regulations and redressing investor grievances", "Preparing the firm's income-tax returns", "Representing the firm before courts in civil suits"], 1, "The compliance officer monitors compliance with securities laws, reports non-compliance to SEBI where observed and handles investor grievances. The officer is also the point of contact for SEBI."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "FIU-IND, which receives reports from reporting entities, functions under:", ["SEBI's Integrated Surveillance Department", "The Reserve Bank of India", "The Ministry of Home Affairs", "The Department of Revenue, Ministry of Finance"], 3, "Financial Intelligence Unit-India is the central agency for receiving, analysing and disseminating financial transaction information and functions under the Department of Revenue."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "Once a designated person makes a continual disclosure of trades to the company, the company must inform the stock exchanges within:", ["The next annual general meeting", "Two trading days of receiving the disclosure", "Thirty days of receiving the disclosure", "Seven calendar days after the end of the quarter"], 1, "The company must notify the stock exchanges of the details within two trading days of receiving the continual disclosure or becoming aware of it."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "During an investigation, SEBI wants to stop a company from issuing further securities until the matter is decided. Which power is it using?", ["Power to pass interim orders pending investigation", "Power to cancel the company's incorporation", "Power to levy a monetary penalty through adjudication", "Power to prosecute the company in a criminal court"], 0, "SEBI can pass interim or ex-parte orders under Sections 11 and 11B, such as restraining an issue, to protect investors while an investigation is pending."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Offshore derivative instruments (ODIs) with Indian securities as underlying may be issued only by:", ["Category I FPIs, subject to conditions", "Indian mutual funds", "Any FPI regardless of category", "SEBI-registered Indian stock brokers only"], 0, "Only Category I FPIs, other than those undertaking certain activities, may issue ODIs, and only to eligible persons after KYC, with reporting to SEBI."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "A broker carries out proprietary trades and client trades. Under SEBI rules, the broker must:", ["Combine proprietary and client trades in one account for efficiency", "Use clients' securities as margin for its own trades", "Keep proprietary and client accounts separate and disclose proprietary trading to clients", "Never trade on its own account under any circumstances"], 2, "Brokers must segregate proprietary and client accounts and inform clients whether they undertake proprietary trading, avoiding misuse of client assets."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "A broker holds client funds at the end of the trading day. Under current SEBI requirements, these client funds must be:", ["Kept in the broker's proprietary account for safety", "Invested in fixed deposits in the broker's own name to earn interest for the firm", "Used to meet the broker's operating expenses", "Upstreamed to the clearing corporation, not retained with the broker or its bank"], 3, "SEBI requires brokers to upstream clients' clear funds to clearing corporations at the end of each day, reducing the risk of misuse of client money."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "A large company stops borrowing from banks and instead raises funds by issuing commercial paper directly to investors. This trend is known as:", ["Monetisation of debt", "Financial repression", "Credit rationing", "Disintermediation"], 3, "Disintermediation means bypassing intermediaries such as banks and tapping savers directly through market instruments like commercial paper or bonds. It reduces the role of banks as intermediaries."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "A custodian receives notice of a rights issue by a company whose shares it holds for a client. It should:", ["Sell the client's shares to avoid the rights issue", "Ignore the notice, as rights issues are optional", "Inform the client promptly and act on the client's instructions", "Subscribe to the rights on its own without asking, using the client's cash"], 2, "Custodians must keep clients informed of corporate actions and act on their instructions, while collecting benefits such as dividends and rights."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "A client opts for quarterly running account settlement. This means the broker must:", ["Settle the client's funds by returning unused credit balance as per the settlement schedule", "Keep the client's funds indefinitely without any settlement", "Transfer the balance to the broker's own account each quarter", "Settle the account only when the client closes it, regardless of any credit balance outstanding"], 0, "Under running account settlement, brokers must periodically pay out clients' unutilised funds on the specified schedule, retaining only what is permitted."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "Which document must a broker give a client when the account is opened, explaining the risks of trading in the securities market?", ["Risk Disclosure Document", "Scheme Information Document", "Offer Document", "Information Memorandum"], 0, "The Risk Disclosure Document, along with the rights and obligations document, is provided at account opening to inform clients of trading risks."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "Under the KRA Regulations, a KRA may use the KYC data it holds:", ["To recommend suitable investment products to investors based on their income details", "To sell investor contact lists to marketing companies", "To publish investor details on its website", "Only for the purposes permitted under the regulations, keeping it confidential"], 3, "KRAs must maintain confidentiality and data security and may not use KYC information for any purpose other than what the regulations permit."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "A company that does not meet the profitability track record under Regulation 6(1) can still make an IPO under Regulation 6(2) if:", ["At least 75% of the net offer is allotted to qualified institutional buyers", "At least 75% of the net offer is allotted to retail investors", "The issue is priced below face value", "Promoters guarantee a minimum listing price to all applicants in the public issue"], 0, "Under Regulation 6(2), a company not meeting 6(1) criteria must allot at least 75% of the net offer to QIBs, with retail allocation capped at 10%."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Can a non-resident Indian (NRI) be a constituent of an FPI?", ["Yes, without any limits whatsoever", "No, NRIs are completely barred from any link with FPIs", "Yes, subject to limits on individual and aggregate contribution and other conditions", "Only if the NRI becomes a resident Indian first"], 2, "NRIs, OCIs and resident Indians may be constituents of FPIs within prescribed contribution limits and conditions, though NRIs cannot register as FPIs themselves."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "A debenture trustee should periodically verify the issuer's asset cover. Asset cover refers to:", ["The number of debenture holders in the issue", "The credit rating assigned to the issuer's parent or holding company by a CRA", "The value of secured assets in relation to the outstanding debentures", "The share price of the issuer company"], 2, "Asset cover shows whether the charged assets are adequate to cover the outstanding principal and interest, and trustees must monitor and report it."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "After a public issue closes, SEBI requires listing and commencement of trading within:", ["Six months of issue closure", "Three working days of issue closure (T+3)", "Thirty days of issue closure", "Fifteen working days after the issue closure"], 1, "SEBI reduced the listing timeline from T+6 to T+3 working days, made mandatory from December 2023, so investors get allotment and refunds faster."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A trader places large buy orders far from the market price and cancels them repeatedly to create a false impression of demand. This practice is known as:", ["Spoofing or layering", "Portfolio rebalancing", "Short covering", "Market making"], 0, "Placing orders without intent to execute, to mislead other participants about demand or supply, is a manipulative practice under PFUTP."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "The Financial Stability and Development Council (FSDC) is chaired by the:", ["Prime Minister of India", "Chairman of SEBI", "Governor of the Reserve Bank of India", "Union Finance Minister"], 3, "The FSDC is an apex co-ordination body for financial sector regulators. It is chaired by the Union Finance Minister and includes heads of RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, PFRDA and IFSCA."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "Which of the following is NOT ordinarily a custodial service?", ["Safekeeping of securities", "Deciding which securities the client should buy", "Informing clients of corporate actions", "Collecting dividends and interest for the client"], 1, "Custodians safeguard assets, settle trades and collect benefits. Investment decisions are made by the client or its fund manager, not the custodian."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "An investor complains against an intermediary on SEBI's SCORES platform. The intermediary is expected to:", ["Wait until SEBI imposes a penalty before responding", "Resolve the complaint and upload an action taken report within the prescribed time", "Ignore it, since complaints on SCORES can be lodged only against listed companies and their RTAs", "Forward it to the stock exchange and take no further action"], 1, "Registered intermediaries must redress complaints received through SCORES within the timelines set by SEBI and upload action taken reports, with escalation if unresolved."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A company shares UPSI with its bankers for evaluating a loan. Under the regulations, such sharing is:", ["Allowed only if the bankers first obtain SEBI's specific written approval for each instance of sharing", "Allowed as a 'legitimate purpose', provided the recipient is given notice to keep it confidential", "Always prohibited, regardless of purpose", "Allowed only after the information is published"], 1, "UPSI may be shared for legitimate purposes, such as with lenders, auditors or advisers, in the ordinary course of business, with confidentiality obligations and database entries."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "Which of the following is a correct statement about a credit rating?", ["It reflects the market price of the security", "It is not a recommendation to buy, sell or hold the instrument", "It is binding on the issuer's lenders when they price their loans", "It guarantees repayment of the debt"], 1, "A rating is an opinion on credit risk only. It is not investment advice, it does not cover market price risk and it is not a guarantee."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "In a research report, an analyst must disclose whether the analyst or associates had actual or beneficial ownership of 1% or more of the subject company's securities. The purpose of this is to:", ["Let readers judge possible conflicts of interest", "Advertise the analyst's personal portfolio", "Inform tax authorities of the analyst's income", "Help the company calculate its public shareholding"], 0, "Disclosure of financial interests and holdings in the subject company helps readers assess whether the recommendation may be biased. Material conflicts must also be disclosed."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A company issues a misleading advertisement about its financial performance to attract investors to its share issue. This falls under PFUTP because:", ["Only stock brokers and their employees can be held liable under PFUTP, not listed companies", "PFUTP applies only to derivative trades", "Publishing misleading information to induce dealing in securities is a prohibited practice", "Advertisements are never regulated by SEBI"], 2, "Making or publishing false or misleading statements to induce investors to deal in securities is a fraudulent practice under PFUTP."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "An RTA registered under Category I can:", ["Act both as a registrar to an issue and as a share transfer agent", "Act only as a registrar to an issue", "Act as a stock broker and depository", "Act only as a share transfer agent"], 0, "Category I RTAs may carry on both activities, while Category II RTAs may act as either a registrar to an issue or a share transfer agent."], ["SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations, 2013", "An investment adviser recommends a high-risk small-cap fund to a retired client whose risk profile shows low risk tolerance. This is a breach of:", ["No rule at all, since the client was always free to refuse the advice given", "Only the client's own responsibility to read documents", "The suitability obligation, as advice must match the client's risk profile", "The adviser's duty to maximise returns at any cost"], 2, "Advisers must ensure that advice is suitable for the client based on risk profiling, and must not recommend products beyond the client's risk capacity."], ["SCRA, 1956 and SCRR, 1957", "Under the SCRR, 1957, minimum public shareholding norms require a listed company to:", ["Bring its public shareholding up to the required level within the time allowed if it falls short", "Keep all public shareholders' holdings in physical form", "Delist automatically from all stock exchanges as soon as its public shareholding falls below 50% of capital", "Ensure promoters always hold at least 75% of the shares"], 0, "Rule 19A requires listed companies to maintain minimum public shareholding and to restore it within the specified period if it falls below, failing which regulatory action follows."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "Under the PFUTP Regulations, 'fraud' includes:", ["Only acts committed by listed companies", "Only acts that cause a combined loss of more than ₹1 crore to investors and are proved by a court of law", "Only acts that are also offences under the Indian Penal Code", "Any act or concealment that induces another person to deal in securities, even without wrongful gain"], 3, "The definition of fraud is deliberately wide and covers acts, omissions and concealment that induce dealing, even without proof of wrongful gain or loss."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A person posts false news on social media about a large order win by a small company to push up its share price and then sells his holdings. This is:", ["A fraudulent practice of spreading false information to manipulate prices", "Legitimate investment research", "Permitted marketing by an investor", "A routine block deal"], 0, "Disseminating misleading information to influence prices and then profiting, often called pump and dump, is expressly prohibited under PFUTP. SEBI can also order disgorgement of the profits made."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "To be registered as a KRA, an applicant must obtain a certificate of registration from:", ["CERSAI", "SEBI", "The stock exchanges jointly", "The Reserve Bank of India"], 1, "KRAs are registered and regulated by SEBI under the KRA Regulations, 2011. CERSAI runs the Central KYC Records Registry, which is a different system."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "A research analyst's compensation should NOT be:", ["Paid as a fixed salary", "Reviewed periodically by a committee", "Based on specific investment banking or brokerage transactions", "Linked to the quality and accuracy of research"], 2, "To maintain objectivity, analysts' pay cannot be tied to specific investment banking or brokerage transactions, though it can reflect research quality."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "Which entity is primarily responsible for carrying out the initial KYC of a client, including obtaining documents and in-person verification where needed?", ["The client's bank, which has already verified the client while opening the savings account", "The KYC Registration Agency", "The SEBI-registered intermediary with whom the client first opens an account", "SEBI"], 2, "The intermediary performs initial KYC and uploads it to the KRA. The KRA stores, validates and makes the record available to other intermediaries."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A distributor sells a high-risk product to an elderly client by describing it as 'capital protected'. Under PFUTP this is:", ["Only a breach of the distributor's internal policy", "Permitted if the product is listed on an exchange", "Mis-selling, which is a fraudulent and unfair trade practice", "Acceptable as long as the client has signed the application form and risk disclosure"], 2, "Mis-selling, including selling unsuitable products or making false or misleading statements, is specifically listed as an unfair trade practice. A signed form does not cure a misleading sales pitch."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "Which of the following best describes the RTA's role in investor grievance redressal?", ["Fixing the dividend rate for the company", "Resolving complaints on transfers, transmission, dividends and records within set timelines", "Deciding appeals against SEBI orders", "Approving changes in the company's articles"], 1, "RTAs handle investor service requests and complaints on behalf of issuers and must address them within set timelines, including those received via SCORES."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "A merchant banker's responsibility for the accuracy of disclosures in an offer document is:", ["Not reduced by the issuer's own responsibility for the contents", "Limited to errors in the price band and the basis of issue price only", "Fully transferred to the issuer once the document is filed", "Applicable only if the issue is undersubscribed"], 0, "Lead managers must independently verify disclosures. The issuer's responsibility does not absolve the merchant banker of its due diligence obligations."], ["SEBI Act, 1992", "Before the SEBI Act, 1992 was passed, SEBI existed as:", ["A non-statutory body set up by a government resolution in 1988", "A wing of the Ministry of Corporate Affairs", "A self-regulatory organisation jointly owned by the major stock exchanges", "A department of the Reserve Bank of India"], 0, "SEBI was first set up in April 1988 through a resolution of the Government of India and was given statutory powers by the SEBI Act, 1992."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "The main benefit of secondary market trading for an investor who bought shares in an IPO is that it:", ["Allows the investor to return the shares to the issuer for a refund", "Guarantees that the shares will always trade above the issue price once they are listed", "Converts the equity shares into fixed-income instruments over time", "Provides liquidity, allowing the investor to exit by selling to another investor"], 3, "The secondary market gives investors an exit route and continuous price discovery. It does not guarantee returns, and shares are not normally returned to the issuer for a refund."], ["Proxy Advisors", "A proxy advisor must share its voting recommendation report with the company concerned:", ["At the same time as it shares the report with its clients", "Only after the shareholder meeting is over", "Never, as the report is confidential to clients", "Only if the company agrees to pay a fee for receiving the report"], 0, "SEBI requires proxy advisors to share reports with the company at the same time as with clients, and to include the company's comments, if any, as an addendum."], ["Proxy Advisors", "If a proxy advisor finds a factual error in a report already sent to clients, it should:", ["Ignore the error, since the report has already been sent and is final", "Delete the report without informing clients", "Issue a correction or clarification to clients promptly", "Wait for the company to point it out publicly"], 2, "Proxy advisors are expected to have procedures for correcting errors and informing clients promptly, maintaining the reliability of their recommendations."], ["Introduction to Compliance", "How does the compliance function differ from internal audit?", ["Compliance only deals with tax laws, while internal audit deals with SEBI rules", "Compliance monitors regulatory adherence on an ongoing basis, while internal audit gives periodic independent assurance on controls", "There is no difference; the two terms mean exactly the same thing", "Compliance work is always outsourced to outside consulting firms, while internal audit can be performed only by the board of directors itself"], 1, "Compliance is an ongoing second-line function, while internal audit provides independent periodic assurance on controls, including on the compliance function itself."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "A promoter wants to trade in his company's shares despite regularly holding UPSI. The regulations allow this through:", ["A trading plan approved by the compliance officer and disclosed to the stock exchanges in advance", "Trading through a relative's account", "Trading only on days when the market is closed", "Verbal permission from the managing director, recorded later in the minutes of the next board meeting"], 0, "A trading plan is a pre-scheduled, publicly disclosed plan that lets persons who perpetually hold UPSI trade without breaching the prohibition, subject to conditions."], ["SEBI (Credit Rating Agencies) Regulations, 1999", "To discourage 'rating shopping', CRAs must disclose:", ["Ratings that were not accepted by the issuer", "The names of their own shareholders' clients", "The personal holdings of the issuer's directors", "The fees charged to each issuer"], 0, "Disclosure of non-accepted ratings prevents issuers from seeking multiple ratings and publishing only the most favourable one. Investors can then see the full rating history of the issuer."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "Insurance companies and insurance intermediaries in India are regulated by:", ["IFSCA", "PFRDA", "IRDAI", "SEBI"], 2, "The Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India, set up under the IRDA Act, 1999, regulates insurers, insurance brokers, agents and other insurance intermediaries."], ["Introduction to the Financial System", "Why are market intermediaries such as brokers, depositories and registrars regulated more strictly than ordinary businesses?", ["They are exempt from company law and need a separate legal framework", "They are not allowed to make profits from their services", "They are owned by the government and must therefore follow all public sector rules on pay and hiring", "They hold client money or securities and their failure can harm investors and market integrity"], 3, "Intermediaries handle investor assets and information, so lapses can cause wide losses and erode trust. That is why registration, capital, conduct and compliance requirements apply to them."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "An investor holds physical shares and wants to hold them electronically. Under the Act, he must:", ["Destroy the certificates and inform the company", "Send the certificates directly to SEBI", "Surrender the certificates to the issuer through a depository participant for dematerialisation", "Sell the shares and buy them again in demat form"], 2, "The investor surrenders physical certificates via the DP, the issuer verifies and cancels them, and the depository credits the securities to the beneficial owner's account."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "A broker executes excessive trades in a client's account mainly to earn more brokerage, without regard to the client's interest. This is called:", ["Algorithmic trading", "Proprietary trading", "Margin trading facility", "Churning, an unfair trade practice"], 3, "Excessive trading in a client account to generate commissions harms the client and is treated as an unfair trade practice by an intermediary."], ["Proxy Advisors", "Institutional investors such as mutual funds use proxy advisors mainly to help them:", ["Analyse resolutions and decide their votes as part of stewardship responsibilities", "Fix the company's dividend policy and recommend the payout ratio to its board each year", "Execute trades in the shares of the company", "Prepare the company's annual accounts"], 0, "Proxy advisors support institutional investors in meeting stewardship obligations by analysing resolutions, though the final voting decision rests with the investor."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "For applying the investment limit, investments by FPIs with common ownership of more than 50% or common control are:", ["Counted only if they are in the same country", "Treated separately, regardless of common ownership", "Clubbed together as one investor group", "Exempt from all limits"], 2, "FPIs having common ownership above 50% or common control are clubbed as an investor group, and the investment limit applies to the group as a whole."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "In a rights issue, an existing shareholder who does not wish to subscribe can:", ["Transfer the rights only to the promoter group at the issue price", "Renounce the rights entitlement in favour of another person", "Demand cash compensation from the company", "Convert the rights into bonus shares"], 1, "Rights entitlements are credited in demat form and can be renounced or traded, so a shareholder who does not subscribe can still realise value."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "Which document does an issuer maintain for securities held in demat form, based on details provided by the depository?", ["Register of charges", "Minutes book of board meetings", "Register of contracts with related parties", "Register and index of beneficial owners"], 3, "Section 11 requires the depository to maintain a register of beneficial owners, which is deemed to be the issuer's register of members for demat holdings."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "Before an offer document is filed, the lead manager must submit to SEBI a:", ["Tax clearance certificate", "No objection certificate from the stock exchange", "Certificate of incorporation", "Due diligence certificate"], 3, "The lead merchant banker certifies that it has exercised due diligence and that disclosures in the offer document are true, fair and adequate."], ["SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations, 2003", "Two connected parties repeatedly buy and sell the same shares between themselves with no real change of ownership. This is called:", ["Wash or circular trading", "Arbitrage trading", "Hedging", "Block deal trading"], 0, "Trades with no genuine change in beneficial ownership create a false appearance of activity and are prohibited as manipulative under the PFUTP Regulations."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "If a beneficial owner suffers a loss due to the negligence of a depository or its participant, under the Act:", ["The depository must indemnify the beneficial owner", "The issuer company must make good the investor's loss", "The investor bears the loss entirely", "SEBI compensates the investor from its own funds"], 0, "Section 16 makes the depository liable to indemnify beneficial owners for losses caused by its own or its participant's negligence."], ["SEBI (Foreign Portfolio Investors) Regulations, 2019", "Under the FPI Regulations, 2019, an FPI's registration is:", ["Permanent, unless suspended or cancelled, subject to payment of fees", "Temporary until approved by Parliament", "Valid only until the FPI makes its first investment in Indian securities", "Valid for one year and must be renewed annually"], 0, "Since 2019, FPI registration is permanent unless suspended or cancelled, provided the FPI pays the applicable fees periodically. Earlier, registration had to be renewed periodically."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "Which of the following would generally be treated as unpublished price sensitive information (UPSI)?", ["Draft quarterly results of a listed company before they are announced", "A press release already posted on the company's website", "An audited annual report already filed with the exchange", "The company's closing share price as published on the stock exchange website"], 0, "UPSI is information not generally available that is likely to materially affect the price, such as financial results, dividends or mergers before public disclosure."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "A reporting entity files an STR on a client. A staff member then tells the client about the report. This is:", ["Tipping off, which is prohibited", "Allowed if the client asks directly", "Good customer service", "Required by law within seven days"], 0, "Disclosing to the client that an STR has been filed is prohibited, as it may help the client evade investigation."], ["SEBI (ICDR) Regulations, 2018", "In a bonus issue, shareholders receive additional shares:", ["At a discount to market price, payable in cash within thirty days", "In exchange for surrendering their debentures", "Without paying any consideration, by capitalising reserves", "Only if they apply within the offer period"], 2, "Bonus shares are issued free of cost by capitalising free reserves or securities premium, in proportion to existing holdings. No money is raised from shareholders in a bonus issue."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "A merchant banker learns price sensitive information about an issuer client during due diligence. It must:", ["Use it to advise its other clients on trading", "Publish it on its website for transparency so that all investors have equal access", "Not use the information for its own trading or share it except as permitted", "Trade quickly before the issue closes"], 2, "Merchant bankers are bound by confidentiality and insider trading rules and must not misuse information obtained from clients. Misuse could breach both PIT and PFUTP rules."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "An intermediary must ensure its key managerial personnel and employees meet the requirements of SEBI. This includes ensuring that:", ["Persons dealing with clients hold the NISM certifications prescribed for their roles", "All employees are relatives of the promoters", "Employees trade only through personal accounts held with competing intermediaries, never with the firm", "Only employees with ten years' experience deal with clients"], 0, "SEBI requires associated persons in specified functions, such as compliance, operations and client dealing, to obtain the relevant NISM certification."], ["SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993", "In a public issue, the registrar to the issue is appointed by:", ["The issuer company, usually in consultation with the lead manager", "The stock exchange where the shares are proposed to be listed after the issue", "SEBI, through a lottery among registered RTAs", "The depository that will hold the shares"], 0, "The issuer appoints the registrar to its issue, normally with input from the lead manager, and enters into an agreement setting out the registrar's duties."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "Under the Custodian Regulations, a custodian must maintain records of its clients' securities and transactions so that:", ["Only the custodian's directors can see them, and not even SEBI inspectors", "SEBI can inspect them and clients can be given accurate statements", "The records can be deleted after each quarter", "The custodian can sell the records to data vendors"], 1, "Custodians must keep accurate and complete records for the prescribed period and make them available to SEBI and clients as required."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "For securities held in a depository, the depository is deemed to be the registered owner. This status allows it to:", ["Vote at company meetings on the shares on behalf of all its account holders", "Receive dividends on the shares for itself", "Effect transfer of ownership on behalf of the beneficial owner", "Sell the shares without the owner's instructions"], 2, "Under Section 10, the depository is the registered owner only to effect transfers. It has no voting or other rights; those belong to the beneficial owner."], ["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "When a sole holder of a demat account dies and has registered a nominee, the securities are:", ["Frozen permanently until a court order is obtained", "Transmitted to the nominee's account after the DP verifies the required documents", "Forfeited to the depository", "Sold and the proceeds paid to the DP"], 1, "On death of a sole holder, securities are transmitted to the registered nominee on submission of the death certificate and transmission request."], ["SEBI (Research Analysts) Regulations, 2014", "A research analyst appears on a television channel and recommends a stock. He must:", ["Guarantee a target price to viewers", "Ask viewers to trade only through his firm so that the trades can be tracked", "Disclose any financial interest or conflict of interest he has in that stock", "Avoid mentioning that he is registered with SEBI"], 2, "Disclosure requirements apply to public appearances too, so viewers know of any holdings or conflicts that may affect the recommendation."], ["Proxy Advisors", "To ensure transparency, a proxy advisor must publicly disclose:", ["The final votes cast by each client", "Its voting recommendation policies and methodology", "The salaries of its analysts", "The personal portfolios of its clients"], 1, "Proxy advisors must disclose their policies, procedures and methodologies for voting recommendations and review them periodically. This lets clients and companies understand how recommendations are reached."], ["SEBI (Prohibition of Insider Trading) Regulations", "Under the Code of Fair Disclosure, a listed company should designate a senior officer to deal with dissemination of information. This officer is called the:", ["Chief Risk Officer", "Principal Officer", "Designated Director", "Chief Investor Relations Officer"], 3, "The code requires a senior officer, the Chief Investor Relations Officer, to oversee uniform and prompt disclosure of UPSI to avoid selective disclosure."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "An underwriter has agreed to underwrite part of an issue. If the issue is undersubscribed and the underwriting is called on, the underwriter must:", ["Ask SEBI to extend the issue indefinitely", "Subscribe for the shortfall in its share within the time stated in the agreement", "Refund all investors' money and ask the issuer to reopen the issue at a lower price", "Withdraw from the agreement without consequences"], 1, "An underwriter's commitment becomes binding when devolvement occurs, and it must subscribe to the unsubscribed portion as per the underwriting agreement."], ["SEBI (Debenture Trustees) Regulations, 1993", "The document setting out the terms on which the debenture trustee holds security for the benefit of debenture holders is the:", ["Underwriting agreement", "Debenture trust deed", "Memorandum of association", "Red herring prospectus"], 1, "The debenture trust deed, executed between the issuer and the trustee, records the security, covenants, and rights and duties of the trustee."], ["SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008", "Which of the following would most likely make an applicant NOT 'fit and proper' for registration?", ["Its promoters have earlier run another registered intermediary without any regulatory action", "A pending charge sheet for an economic offence against its director", "Its directors hold postgraduate degrees", "It has filed audited accounts every year"], 1, "Criminal complaints or charge sheets for economic offences, debarment orders and similar matters are disqualifications under the fit and proper criteria."], ["Prevention of Money Laundering Act, 2002", "A client who is a politically exposed person (PEP) opens a trading account. Under SEBI's AML guidelines, the intermediary should:", ["Refuse the account in all cases", "Open the account without any additional checks", "Apply simplified due diligence because PEPs are well known", "Apply enhanced due diligence and obtain senior management approval for the relationship"], 3, "PEPs are high-risk clients, so intermediaries must perform enhanced due diligence, verify source of funds and get senior management approval to establish or continue the relationship."], ["Depositories Act, 1996", "Under the Depositories Act, a depository must be:", ["A department of the Reserve Bank of India that is constituted under the Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "A trust registered with the Charity Commissioner", "A partnership firm owned by brokers", "A company registered under the Companies Act and holding a SEBI certificate of registration"], 3, "A depository is defined as a company formed and registered under the Companies Act that has been granted a certificate of registration by SEBI."], ["SEBI (Depositories and Participants) Regulations, 2018", "A DP's books show a client holding of 500 shares, while the depository's records show 450. The DP should:", ["Identify and resolve the difference through reconciliation immediately", "Treat its own books as correct and ignore the depository", "Credit 50 shares from another client's account", "Wait until the client complains and only then check the depository's records"], 0, "Daily reconciliation between DP records and depository records ensures accuracy, and any difference must be investigated and corrected promptly. Unresolved differences can put client assets at risk."], ["SEBI (Merchant Bankers) Regulations etc.", "Which intermediary collects application money from investors in a public issue and handles refunds?", ["Custodian", "Banker to an issue", "Debenture trustee", "Credit rating agency"], 1, "Bankers to an issue accept applications and money, maintain escrow and public issue accounts, and handle refunds in coordination with the registrar."], ["SEBI (KYC Registration Agency) Regulations, 2011", "Which of the following is the main benefit of KRAs to intermediaries?", ["Reduced duplication of KYC work and access to a uniform, verified KYC record", "Freedom from having to know their clients at all", "Exemption from all anti-money laundering obligations", "A guarantee from SEBI against client fraud"], 0, "KRAs save repeated KYC effort and provide uniform records, but intermediaries still remain responsible for due diligence and AML obligations."], ["SEBI (Stock Brokers) Regulations", "Instead of a broad power of attorney, a client may now authorise a broker to transfer securities for settlement and pledging through a:", ["Demat Debit and Pledge Instruction (DDPI)", "Letter of indemnity", "Delivery Instruction Slip signed in blank", "General power of attorney covering all assets"], 0, "SEBI introduced DDPI to limit the broker's authority to specific purposes such as settlement and margin pledge, replacing broad powers of attorney."], ["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996", "A custodian must reconcile client holdings with depository records regularly in order to:", ["Increase its fee income from clients", "Reduce the net worth it needs to maintain", "Avoid informing clients of corporate actions", "Detect and correct any mismatch in clients' securities promptly"], 3, "Regular reconciliation ensures that the custodian's books match depository records and that client assets are accounted for correctly. Breaks must be investigated and reported promptly."], ["Regulatory Framework – General View", "A company wants to set up a finance business in GIFT City's International Financial Services Centre. Which regulator will primarily supervise it?", ["Securities and Exchange Board of India", "International Financial Services Centres Authority (IFSCA)", "Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority", "Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India"], 1, "IFSCA is the unified regulator for financial products, services and institutions in an IFSC such as GIFT City. It took over the powers that SEBI, RBI, IRDAI and PFRDA would otherwise exercise there."]]};
